### Proposed Architecture

#### 1. Package placement and injection

**`internal/vcs` (new).** Imports only the standard library; does not import `task`.

`vcs.New(root, tasksDir string) *Repo` runs no git. On first use, `Check()` resolves and caches:
- `top`: the output of `git -C <root> rev-parse --show-toplevel`.
- `tasksRel`: the tasks directory relative to `top`, but only when it lies inside `top`. It becomes the exclude pathspec `:(top,exclude)<tasksRel>`. When the tasks directory is outside the worktree, there is no exclusion.

Every command runs as `git -C <top> …`.

How commands are run:
- `exec.CommandContext` with a 60-second per-command timeout and `Stdin = nil`.
- Environment: `os.Environ()` plus `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and `LC_ALL=C`.
- Errors read `git <args>: <trimmed stderr>`. A missing binary maps to `ErrGitNotFound`.

**Identity.** `vcs.ResolveIdentity(root string) Identity` returns `Identity{Name string; FromGitEmail bool}`.
1. Run `git -C <root> config --get user.email`. This works outside a repo too, because it also reads the global config.
2. Take the local part (before `@`) and sanitize it:
   - lowercase
   - replace every rune outside `[a-z0-9._-]` with `-`
   - collapse repeated `-`
   - trim leading and trailing `.` and `-`
   - replace `..` with `.`
   - strip a trailing `.lock`
3. If the email is missing, git is missing, or the result is empty, fall back to `os/user.Current().Username` (take the part after any `\`), then `$USER`, then `$USERNAME`, then `default`. Sanitize the fallback the same way. `FromGitEmail` is then false.

`GitRepo` methods, implemented by `*vcs.Repo`:

| Method | Git |
|---|---|
| `Check() error` | Git is on PATH. `rev-parse --show-toplevel` works. `--is-bare-repository` is false. None of `MERGE_HEAD`, `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD`, `REVERT_HEAD`, `rebase-merge`, `rebase-apply` or `BISECT_LOG` exists (checked with `rev-parse --git-path`). `ls-files -u` is empty. `var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT` works. The tasks directory must not be `top` itself or an ancestor of it. |
| `Head() (branch, sha string, err error)` | `symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD`; a detached HEAD returns `ErrDetachedHead`. Then `rev-parse HEAD`. |
| `ResolveCommit(rev) (string, error)` | `rev-parse --verify --quiet <rev>^{commit}` |
| `IsAncestor(a, b) (bool, error)` | `merge-base --is-ancestor` (exit code 1 means false) |
| `BranchSHA(name) (sha string, ok bool, err error)` | `rev-parse --verify --quiet refs/heads/<name>` |
| `FirstExistingBranch(names []string) (string, string, error)` | `BranchSHA` for each name in order |
| `BranchAvailable(name) error` | `check-ref-format --branch`. The ref must not exist. No proper prefix of it may exist as a ref. `for-each-ref --count=1 refs/heads/<name>/` must be empty. |
| `DirtyPaths() ([]string, error)` | `status --porcelain=v1 -z --untracked-files=all -- . <exclude>` |
| `IndexTree() (string, error)` | `write-tree`, a snapshot of the real index used for rollback |
| `Snapshot(msg string) (sha string, committed bool, err error)` | With a temporary `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: `read-tree HEAD`, then `add -A -- . <exclude>`, then `write-tree`. If the tree equals HEAD's tree, stop without committing. Otherwise `commit-tree <tree> -p HEAD -F -`, then `update-ref <HEAD's branch ref> <new> <old>` (compare-and-swap), then `reset -q -- . <exclude>` so real-index entries outside the tasks directory match the new HEAD. Entries under the tasks directory stay exactly as the user left them. |
| `TreeOf(rev) (string, error)` | `rev-parse <rev>^{tree}` |
| `MergeTrees(base, ours, theirs) (tree string, conflicts []string, err error)` | `merge-tree --write-tree --name-only --merge-base=<base> <ours> <theirs>`; exit code 1 means conflicts, with the paths listed |
| `CommitTree(tree, parent, msg) (string, error)` | `commit-tree <tree> -p <parent> -F -` |
| `Replay(onto, upstream, branch string) (newTip string, conflicts []string, err error)` | `git replay` in print mode (on current git, `--ref-action=print`; confirm against the installed git's `git replay -h` during implementation) with `--onto <onto> <upstream>..<branch>`. The `update refs/heads/<branch> <new> <old>` line is parsed and the ref is **not** moved. On a conflict, `CONFLICT … in <path>` lines are parsed; the raw stderr is used if none are found. Replaying a merge commit is reported as an error. The worktree and index are never touched. |
| `CreateBranch(name, sha) error` | `update-ref refs/heads/<name> <sha> ""` (fails if the ref exists) |
| `MoveBranch(name, newSHA, oldSHA) error` | `update-ref refs/heads/<name> <new> <old>` (compare-and-swap) |
| `DeleteBranch(name, expectSHA) error` | `update-ref -d refs/heads/<name> <expect>` |
| `Switch(name) error` | `switch <name>`. Git checks this atomically and refuses without changing anything if local changes would be overwritten. |
| `ResetKeep(sha) error` | `reset --keep <sha>`, used when the rebased branch is the one checked out. Atomic refusal if it would conflict with local changes. |
| `RestoreIndex(tree) error` | `read-tree <tree>` |

**`internal/task/git.go` (new)** defines:
- `GitRepo`: the interface above, exported so external test packages can wrap it for fault injection.
- `Identity{Name string; FromGitEmail bool}`.
- `CurrentTaskStore`.
- `type ServiceOption func(*Service)`, with `WithGit(GitRepo)`, `WithIdentity(Identity)` and `WithCurrentTaskStore(CurrentTaskStore)`.

`NewService(…, cfg *config.Config, opts ...ServiceOption)` becomes variadic, so no caller or mock has to change.

**`project.Build`** always passes:
- `WithCurrentTaskStore(mdStorage)`
- `WithIdentity(vcs.ResolveIdentity(cfg.Resolution.Root))`. This is one fast git call at build time, which is already lazy (first tool call or CLI command).

When `cfg.Git.Branching` is set, it also passes `WithGit(vcs.New(root, tasksDir))`. `Initialize` runs no git. A non-repo project fails only on the first `start_task`, with a clear error.

**Locking.** All git steps run inside `StartTask` and `CompleteTask` while `s.mu` is held. That makes the ref changes and the record writes atomic with respect to every other tool call and every web read. Web handlers only read through locked service methods, so they wait (typically well under a second, bounded by the per-command timeout) and never see a half-applied state. Nothing in the web layer can reach git.

#### 2. Config

In `internal/config/config.go`:

```go
// GitConfig holds the opt-in git branch-per-task workflow.
type GitConfig struct {
    // Branching makes start_task/complete_task create, squash, rebase and
    // switch per-task branches in the code repository. Off by default.
    Branching bool `yaml:"branching"`
    // BaseBranches is the priority list of mainline branches; a top-level
    // task branches from the first one that exists. _patched variants rank
    // above plain ones.
    BaseBranches []string `yaml:"base_branches"`
}
// Config gains: Git GitConfig `yaml:"git"`
var DefaultBaseBranches = []string{"main_patched", "master_patched", "main", "master"}
```

- `DefaultConfig` returns `Git{Branching: false, BaseBranches: DefaultBaseBranches}`.
- `applyDefaults` trims each entry and drops empty ones. If the list ends up empty, it refills it with `DefaultBaseBranches`. `Branching` is a bool and is left as written.
- Env override: `EnvGitBranching = "MCP_GIT_BRANCHING"`, a bool parsed like `MCP_WEB_ENABLED` (an unparseable value is ignored). It is added to `IsolateEnv` and to the CLAUDE.md env table. There is no env override for the base branch list.
- This repo's `mcp-tasks.yaml` gets a commented reference block (`git: {branching: false, base_branches: [...]}`). Branching is not enabled by this task.

#### 3. Naming (pure functions in `internal/task/branching.go`, tested in-package)

- `slugRe = ^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$`, maximum 48 characters.
- `slugify(s)`: lowercase ASCII; every other rune becomes `-`; collapse repeats; trim; cut to at most 40 characters at a hyphen.

`shortName(t)`:
1. If `t.ID` matches `slugRe`, contains a letter and is not `wip`, use the id.
2. Otherwise use `slugify(ID) + "-" + slugify(Title)`, dropping empty parts. For example, id `8` becomes `8-add-login-form`.
3. If that is empty or equals `wip`, use `task-` plus the first 8 hex characters of `sha1(ID)`.

Branch names:
- `wipBranch(user, name) = user + "/wip/" + name`
- `subWipBranch(parentBranch, sub) = parentBranch + "--" + sub`
- `finalBranch(wip)`: split at the first `/wip/` and return `user/rest`. It is derived from the stored `branch`, so a later change of the email cannot desynchronize it.

Why no ref can sit inside another:
- Wip branches are always at depth 3.
- Final branches are at depth 2, with a name other than `wip`.
- A subtask wip is a sibling of its parent's wip, not a child.
- The remaining hazards are branches that already exist, such as a branch literally named `<user>` or a foreign `<user>/<name>`. `BranchAvailable` catches them.

#### 4. Task model and storage

**New fields on `task.Task`.** Each has `yaml:"…,omitempty" json:"…,omitempty"`.

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `Branch` | The wip branch |
| `BaseBranch` | The base branch for a top-level task, or the parent's wip for a subtask |
| `StartCommit` | The commit the wip branch currently sits on. Rewritten on restart. |
| `FinalBranch` | Set on a delivered top-level completion |
| `SquashCommit` | The final commit (top-level) or the commit merged into the parent's wip (subtask) |

Where the fields go:
- `markdown.go`: added to both the `Save` struct and the `parse` struct.
- `IndexEntry` and its converters: `Branch`, `FinalBranch` and `SquashCommit`. The board needs them, and the parent gate reads subtasks from the index.
- `BaseBranch` and `StartCommit` stay out of the index. Only the detail view and the flows need them, and the flows load full tasks.
- Branch fields **survive a reopen**: `update` does not clear them. `Resolution`, `ResolutionNote` and `ClosedAt` are still cleared as before.
- They are set only through an unexported update option, `withBranch(branchInfo)`, so `update` stays the single persistence path.
- New `WithCommitMessage(string) UpdateOption`. `update` ignores it; `CompleteTask` reads it.

**Pointer store**, in `internal/storage/current.go` (new):
- `UsersDirName = ".users"` and `CurrentTaskFileName = "current_task"`.
- MarkdownStorage methods:
  - `CurrentTaskPath(user) string`
  - `ReadCurrentTask(user) (id string, ok bool, err error)`: the trimmed first line
  - `WriteCurrentTask(user, id) error`: `MkdirAll`, then an atomic write via tmp file and rename
  - `RemoveCurrentTask(user) error`: removing an absent file is fine
- `LoadAll` and `isStaleOnDisk` skip `.users` explicitly, next to `archive`, so a stray `.users/.users.md` can never be read as a task.
- `.users` is added to the reserved custom ids in `storage/files.go`. `.current_task` is **not** reserved.

**Legacy root `.current_task`.** Nothing reads or writes it any more, and it stays ignored by `.gitignore:20`. There are no `.gitignore` changes.

#### 5. Operation sequences

**Shared machinery.**
- `gitTxn{undo []func() error}` with `add` and `rollback()`. Rollback runs the undos in reverse and joins their errors.
- `captureTask(id)` and `capturePointer()` snapshot state; their undos rewrite exactly what was captured, including "absent".
- Every flow captures `origBranch, origSHA := Head()` and `origIndex := IndexTree()`.
- Only the final step changes the worktree: a `Switch` or a `ResetKeep`. Everything before it touches refs, the index, or files under the tasks directory. Those are undone exactly by `MoveBranch`/`DeleteBranch`, `RestoreIndex(origIndex)` and the record/pointer restores. The index restore is needed after a snapshot.
- If rollback itself fails, the error names every branch and SHA involved and says that no content was lost. Everything stays reachable through the wip branch, the snapshot commits and the reflog.
- After every successful switch, the flow calls `s.index.Load()`. It does not rely on mtime self-healing. This matters when the tasks directory is tracked in the code repo and trees differ.

Key: **P** = preflight (no mutation); **M** = mutation, with its undo in [brackets].

**5.1 Leaving the current HEAD (`vacate`).** This check is used by fresh start, restart and subtask start. Changes under the tasks directory are ignored and simply carried along; they are never dirty for this purpose. The rule:

- `DirtyPaths()` is empty: nothing to do.
- Dirty, and HEAD is the `Branch` of a task that is `in_progress` (looked up in the index; this includes the task being started): **M** `Snapshot("wip(<id>): checkpoint before <operation>")` on that server-owned wip branch. [undo: `MoveBranch(branch, origSHA, W)`, then `RestoreIndex`]
- Dirty, HEAD equals the chosen start-point branch, and this is a fresh start that branches off exactly that branch (for example HEAD is on `main_patched`): the changes are carried by the switch. This is the familiar "edit on main, then start the task" flow.
- Otherwise HEAD is a user branch that is not the start point. **P** refuses with: `uncommitted changes on <branch> (…up to 20 paths); commit or stash them before starting <id>`. The server never commits on user branches and never guesses where foreign changes belong.

A detached HEAD is refused in every flow.

**5.2 `start_task`: fresh top-level T** (no `Branch`, or `Branch` set but its ref no longer exists; status `todo`)

- **P0.** The existing checks: T is `todo` and not blocked.
- **P1.** `Check()`. `Identity.FromGitEmail` must be true, otherwise the error is "git config user.email is not set; it names your branches".
- **P2.** Choose `base, baseTip := FirstExistingBranch(cfg.Git.BaseBranches)`. If none exists: "none of the base branches [..] exist; set git.base_branches".
- **P3.** Compute `wip` and `final`. Run `BranchAvailable(wip)`. For `final`: it must be available, or it must equal `T.FinalBranch` (a task this server completed before). Otherwise: "branch <final> exists and was not created by task T".
- **P4.** The `vacate` classification from 5.1.

Mutations:
- **M1.** `vacate`, if it needs a checkpoint commit.
- **M2.** `CreateBranch(wip, baseTip)`. [undo: `DeleteBranch(wip, baseTip)`]
- **M3.** `update(T → in_progress, withBranch{Branch: wip, BaseBranch: base, StartCommit: baseTip})`. [undo: restore record]
- **M4.** `WriteCurrentTask(user, T)`. [undo: restore pointer]
- **M5.** `Switch(wip)`, then `index.Load()`.

Why the base list wins over "the current HEAD": after a delivered completion the user is usually still on the previous task's final branch. Branching from the first existing base means the next task never stacks on the previous one, and nobody has to switch by hand.

**5.3 `start_task`: fresh subtask S of parent P**

- P has no `Branch` (a legacy parent, or branching was off when it started): start S exactly as today (auto-start the parent, write the status) and write the pointer. No git.
- P is `todo` while branching is enabled: first run 5.2 steps M2–M4 for P, in the same journal but without the switch. Then continue below using P's new wip.
- P is `in_progress` with a `Branch`:
  - **P.** `Check`, identity, `BranchSHA(P.Branch)` must exist, `subWip` must be available, and the `vacate` rule. If HEAD is P's wip and it is dirty, the checkpoint goes onto P's wip, so S starts from P's latest work.
  - **M1.** `vacate`. Then read `Ptip`.
  - **M2.** `CreateBranch(subWip, Ptip)`.
  - **M3.** `update(S → in_progress, withBranch{Branch: subWip, BaseBranch: P.Branch, StartCommit: Ptip})`, then write the pointer as S.
  - **M4.** `Switch(subWip)`, then `index.Load()`.

**5.4 `start_task`: restart** (T has a `Branch` whose ref exists, and T is either `todo`, i.e. reopened, or `in_progress`; HEAD can be anywhere)

- **P.** `Check` and identity.
- **P.** Choose the parent line:
  - Top-level: `onto` is `T.BaseBranch` if that branch still exists, otherwise the first existing entry of `base_branches`.
  - Subtask: `onto = P.Branch`. If P is `todo`, restart or start P first, in the same journal. If P has no branch: "parent P has no branch; start it first".
- **P.** `ontoTip := BranchSHA(onto)`, `oldTip := BranchSHA(T.Branch)`. `ResolveCommit(T.StartCommit)` must succeed and `IsAncestor(T.StartCommit, oldTip)` must hold. If not, the error says the wip history no longer contains its recorded start.
- **P.** The `vacate` rule. Changes that are uncommitted while HEAD is T's own wip count as server-owned and get checkpointed.

Mutations:
- **M1.** `vacate`. If HEAD was T's wip, `oldTip` becomes the checkpoint commit.
- **M2.** If `ontoTip != T.StartCommit`: `newTip, conflicts := Replay(ontoTip, T.StartCommit, T.Branch)`.
  - On conflict, **fail** with "rebasing <T.Branch> onto <onto> conflicts in: a.go, b.go; nothing was changed". Full rollback: the checkpoint is undone. Nothing else was touched: `git replay` leaves no in-progress rebase state, so no `rebase --abort` is needed.
  - Otherwise `newTip = oldTip`.
- **M3.** `MoveBranch(T.Branch, newTip, oldTip)`. [undo: `MoveBranch(T.Branch, oldTip, newTip)`]
  - Special case: if HEAD is `T.Branch`, skip this step and do `ResetKeep(newTip)` in M6 instead. Moving the checked-out ref behind git's back would leave a reverse diff in the worktree.
- **M4.** `update(T → in_progress, withBranch{…, BaseBranch: onto, StartCommit: ontoTip})`. This keeps a later squash at "base + one commit". `FinalBranch` and `SquashCommit` are kept as history. [undo: restore]
- **M5.** Write the pointer as T. [undo: restore]
- **M6.** `Switch(T.Branch)`, or `ResetKeep(newTip)` when HEAD was already T's branch [undo: `ResetKeep(oldTip)`]. Then `index.Load()`.

`start_task` on an `in_progress` task **without** a branch keeps today's error ("not in todo status").

**5.5 `complete_task`, delivered, top-level T with a `Branch`**

- **P0.** The existing checks: `in_progress`, and no subtask with status other than done.
- **P1.** `Check`, identity, and HEAD must be `T.Branch` ("switch to <branch> first").
- **P2.** `ResolveCommit(T.StartCommit)` must succeed and `IsAncestor(T.StartCommit, HEAD)` must hold.
- **P3.** The final branch: absent, which means create it; or it exists and `T.FinalBranch == final`, which means move it with compare-and-swap. Anything else is an error.
- **P4.** The subtask gate (5.7).
- **P5.** Compute `msg` (section 7).

Mutations:
- **M1.** `W := Snapshot("wip(T): snapshot before completion")`. The wip branch keeps every commit plus W. [undo: `MoveBranch(T.Branch, origSHA, W)` and `RestoreIndex(origIndex)`]
- **M2.** `C := CommitTree(TreeOf(W), T.StartCommit, msg)`. This only creates objects, so there is nothing to undo.
- **M3.** `CreateBranch(final, C)` [undo: `DeleteBranch(final, C)`], or `MoveBranch(final, C, oldFinal)` [undo: move it back].
- **M4.** `update(T → done, opts…, withBranch{…, FinalBranch: final, SquashCommit: C})`. [undo: restore]
- **M5.** Pointer: if it names T, remove it. [undo: restore]
- **M6.** `Switch(final)`. The worktree is clean outside the tasks directory, and `final`'s tree equals W's tree, so this cannot conflict. Then `index.Load()`.

Result:
- `final` = `start_commit` + exactly one commit, whose tree is W's.
- Task records are not in it. They were never in any server commit; only whatever the user committed themselves is.
- The repository ends on `final`.

**5.6 `complete_task`, delivered, subtask S with a `Branch`**

- **P.** As 5.5 P0–P2 for S. `Ptip := BranchSHA(P.Branch)` must exist. Compute `msg`.

Mutations:
- **M1.** `W := Snapshot(…)` on S's wip. [undo as above]
- **M2.** `M, conflicts := MergeTrees(S.StartCommit, Ptip, W)`. Conflicts **fail** and trigger a full rollback that lists the paths.
- **M3.** `C := CommitTree(M, Ptip, msg)`.
- **M4.** `MoveBranch(P.Branch, C, Ptip)`. The compare-and-swap protects against a concurrent move of the parent's wip. [undo: move it back]
- **M5.** `update(S → done, …, withBranch{…, SquashCommit: C})`. [undo: restore]
- **M6.** Pointer: if it names S, set it to P. [undo: restore]
- **M7.** `Switch(P.Branch)`, then `index.Load()`.

The parent is **not** auto-completed when `P.Branch != ""`. It stays `in_progress` on its wip until `complete_task(P)` produces P's final branch. S's wip branch stays as history.

**5.7 Parent completion gate** (a git check, evaluated before any mutation of a delivered P completion). For every subtask `s` of P, read from the index (records are branch-independent, so the index is authoritative):

1. `s.Status == done`. This is the existing rule and covers subtasks that are started but unfinished, and never-started `todo` subtasks.
2. If `s.Branch != ""` and `s.EffectiveResolution().Delivered()`:
   - `s.SquashCommit` must be non-empty.
   - `IsAncestor(s.SquashCommit, BranchSHA(P.Branch))` must hold.

   If it fails: "subtask s was completed but its work (<short sha>) is not in <P.Branch>; it may have been reset away. Restart s or merge it back."
3. A subtask that has a branch but was closed as non-delivered needs nothing: its abandoned work stays on its own wip.
4. A done, delivered subtask without a branch (legacy, or done directly on P's wip) is accepted as-is.

**5.8 `complete_task`, non-delivered** (`obsolete|superseded|duplicate|wontfix`)

**Case A: T has no `Branch`, or `T.Branch` is not checked out.** Record-only, as today, including the cascade. No git runs, and any wip branch is left untouched. Pointer: if it names T, it moves to the parent (for a subtask whose parent is still open) or is removed.

**Case B: `T.Branch` is HEAD.**

Preflight **P**:
- `Check`.
- Choose the return target:
  - Top-level: `T.BaseBranch` if it exists, otherwise the first existing entry of `base_branches`.
  - Subtask: `P.Branch`, which must exist.
  - If none is found, the error is "no branch to return to…".

Mutations:
- **M1.** `W := Snapshot("wip(T): closed as <resolution>")`. This is the safety commit: all uncommitted code work is preserved on the wip branch. [undo]
- **M2.** The existing cascade `closeSubtasksWith` (records only; the subtasks' own wip branches are untouched), then `update(T → done, resolution, note)`. [undo: restore every touched record]
- **M3.** Pointer: if it names T, set it to P (subtask) or remove it. [undo]
- **M4.** `Switch(target)`, then `index.Load()`.

Nothing has to be carried back: the records live outside the code branches.

For a parent with a branch, the cascade never auto-completes it. Last-subtask auto-completion is also skipped when `P.Branch != ""`.

**5.9 Pointer on disabled mode and legacy tasks.** Pointer writes follow the same rules everywhere:
- start writes it;
- delivered or non-delivered completion moves it to the parent or removes it, but only if it names the completed task.

With branching disabled, this pointer is the only new side effect. There is no git.

**5.10 `update_task` guard.** It lives in the exported `Update`, after the lock is taken and before `update`, and only applies when `s.git != nil`:

| Transition | Result |
|---|---|
| `status: in_progress` from `todo` | Refused: "git branching is enabled; use start_task" |
| Closing (`status: done`, or `resolution`) an `in_progress` task that has a `Branch` | Refused: "use complete_task" |
| `done → in_progress` for a task with a `Branch` | Refused |
| Reopening to `todo` | Allowed; branch fields are kept, so a later `start_task` takes the restart path |

`testsupport.Seed` is unaffected because test backlogs have no git.

**5.11 Auto-archive.** No change. `completeTaskHandler` still calls `RunAutoArchive()`, and the startup run stays. Archive renames only touch the tasks directory, which no server commit includes.

**5.12 CLI.**
- `cmdStart` and `cmdComplete` share the service and run the same flows.
- `complete` gains `-m/--message`, which passes `WithCommitMessage`.
- Table output prints `Branch:` (or `Final branch:`) when one is set. JSON includes the fields automatically.

